From: JamesDSP root fork - 0006
Subject: [PATCH 0006] engine: async initial engine build on EffectCreate
 (passthrough priming, builder swap-in)

The user-reported symptom this patch addresses (xaga, MTK HyperOS / Android 15):
"音效生效有延迟，需要等几秒才能生效" together with switch glitches that grow
with the number of mounted sessions.

Root cause: EffectCreate built the initial engine synchronously on the
creating thread - an audioserver binder thread for a factory create.  The
build is jdsp_engine_new() PLUS the full jdsp_state_replay() of the persisted
state (26 recorded commands on this device), which includes the
InterpolatingEq minimum-phase FFT design and the convolver/impulse reloads.
Every attach of every session therefore stalled a binder thread of
audioserver for the whole replay (60-230 ms measured per attach on xaga, and
N sessions attach serially), and the same work re-ran inside the HAL for the
watcher's re-attach bursts when the global switch went on.

Change (bounded by the machinery 0003/0005 already put in place):

  - EffectCreate publishes the instance *pristine* (a freshly constructed
    engine, no replay) when state persistence is active, and the builder
    thread builds its real engine and publishes it through the existing
    `pending` swap (crossfaded at a buffer boundary by the 0005 accumulate
    path).  Without persistence (jdsp_state_enabled() == 0) the historical
    synchronous path is kept verbatim.

  - A pristine instance renders a raw passthrough (jdsp_passthrough mirrors
    the 0005 accumulate contract but copies the input instead of running the
    zeroed DSP, whose postGain/limiter are still unset and would not be safe
    to run).

  - While pristine, chunked transfers (impulse/string "done" commands: the
    heavy FFT/parsing work) are recorded to the state store and replied with
    success instead of being executed inline on the command thread; the
    builder's replay applies them for real.  Value parameters keep queuing
    through the 0003 deferred queue and are drained into the builder's
    engine at the swap.

  - The generation bookkeeping keeps pristine instances stale
    (jdsp_sync_generations and the command-path cur_gen bumps skip them), so
    the first parameter burst of an attach can never leave an instance in
    passthrough forever.

  - The builder's staleness check treats stateReplayed == 0 (pristine) as
    always stale; a successful build/replay sets stateReplayed = 1 (also set
    by the synchronous fallback path, so the flag is meaningful with and
    without persistence).

Semantics kept unchanged: 0003 queue semantics, 0005 accumulate semantics,
the write lock, and the recorded state format are untouched; a swap still
crossfades exactly one buffer, and the previous engine is retired to the
builder thread for teardown.
